# Real device bytes, captured passively. Regression vectors for StreamParser.
#
# Origin:  RCT Power DC 10.0, firmware 2.3.5687, captured 2026-10-02 from two
#          passive listening sessions (45 s and 55 s). Not a single byte was sent
#          to the device: 0 reads, 0 writes, 0 periodic registrations. The frames
#          are the device's answers to a foreign client, mirrored to all listeners.
#
# Content: one raw byte sequence per non-comment line, exactly as it appeared on
#          the wire (escaped, starting with the start byte 0x2B for frames). Each
#          sequence is introduced by a '# (x) ...' comment and is loaded by tag in
#          tests/test_stream_device_frames.py.
#
# Anonymisation: only LONG RESPONSE frames (0x06) are taken. They carry nothing but
#          logger timestamps and float values. The short 0x05 frames of the capture
#          are deliberately NOT included because they carry the serial number and the
#          device name (object ids 0x7924ABD9, 0xC0A7074F, 0x16A1F844, 0x68BC034D).
#          Every sequence was checked for printable ASCII runs of four characters or
#          more; a run found this way is replaced by 'X' of the same length, the CRC
#          is recomputed with app.protocol.crc.crc16_ccitt and the body is re-escaped
#          with app.protocol.escaping.escape_body.
#
# ASCII check result: (a) held two runs of four bytes each, ')`>?' and '(4>S', both
#          float and timestamp bytes rather than text; they were replaced as described
#          above, so (a) is a real device frame with four payload bytes substituted and
#          a recomputed CRC. Its declared length (16) and its measured body length (32)
#          are the originals, which is what the regression test exercises. (b) to (g)
#          held no run and are byte-identical to the capture. The replacement bytes
#          themselves form two runs of four 'X', which carry no information.
#
# The CRC of (a) to (e) verifies over the measured body, never over the declared one.

# (a) LONG RESPONSE, object id 0x5293B668, capture 1 raw offset 978.
#      Declared length 16, measured body 32 bytes, 28 bytes of payload. ASCII runs [')`>?', '(4>S'] replaced by 'X', CRC recomputed, body re-escaped.
2b0600105293b6686ac0a7046ac058585858f0406ac058585858aae56ac027083e68d4908cc0

# (b) LONG RESPONSE, object id 0x9247DB99, capture 2 raw offset 2423.
#      Declared length 8, measured body 24 bytes, 20 bytes of payload.
2b0600089247db996ac0a9826ac02d2bb83e5697096ac02a8c4211c0fbb86b

# (c) LONG RESPONSE, object id 0xCBDAD315, capture 1 raw offset 1239.
#      Declared length 488, measured body 16 bytes, 12 bytes of payload.
2b0601e8cbdad3156ac0a7046ac02960000000043aa8

# (d) LONG RESPONSE, object id 0xCBDAD315, capture 2 raw offset 2575.
#      Declared length 488, measured body 8 bytes, 4 bytes of payload.
2b0601e8cbdad3156ac0a98283e7

# (e) LONG RESPONSE, object id 0x3906A1D0, capture 1 raw offset 1301.
#      Declared length 480, measured body 480 bytes, 476 bytes of payload.


# (f) Serial-bridge burst, capture 2 raw offset 978, 26 bytes, no frame at all:
#      '+++' followed by CR and UART framing noise. The separator null that follows
#      it in the capture is listed separately as (g).
2b2b2b0d2b2b2bf8781e78fe781e78fe781ef89e869e869e86e9

# (g) A single separator null byte as it precedes a frame in the stream.
00
